# What data/CARD_MANIFEST.sha256 covers, and what it does not
#
# 178 of the 584 files in the 2026-09-30 card-corpus snapshot:
#   data/cards              144   the training corpus, every lo::/hi::/cell_*/pooled_* figure
#   data/prospective_cards   34   the prospective headline and every prosp_* key
#
# NOT COVERED, 406 files feeding 29 registry keys:
#   out/publisher_cards     260   the publisher census, 14 pub_* keys
#   out/lora_cards          136   the kurtosis/LoRA analysis, 11 lk_* keys
#   out/qwen35_cards         10   4 qwen35* keys
# Those three sets are pinned only inside the snapshot archive, which is
# outside git, so a clone cannot verify them. Recorded rather than implied
# covered.
#
# 2026-10-13: as far as a clone can go, it now goes. The 406 card files
# cannot be hashed from a checkout because they are not in one. What IS in a
# checkout is the artifact each of those card sets was reduced to, and that
# artifact is what the registry reads:
#   out/publisher_census.json        -> 14 pub_*    keys
#   out/track2_lowrank.json          -> 11 lk_*     keys
#   data/qwen35/published_rows.csv   -> 2  qwen35_* keys
#   data/qwen35/published_rejects.csv-> 2  qwen35_* keys
# Those four are pinned in data/DERIVED_INPUTS.sha256 and checked by
# tools/pin_derived_inputs.py. One unverifiable hop remains -- cards to
# artifact -- and it is now named rather than standing in for the whole
# chain. The 29 keys and the figures they carry are listed in PROVENANCE.md
# under the 2026-10-13 entry.
#
# ORDERING -- FIXED 2026-10-13. Both re-harvest tests now call
# _assert_card_corpus_matches_manifest_first() before reading a single card,
# so a corpus that fails its manifest can no longer coexist with a
# re-harvest that passes. Replayed: corrupt one pinned card and the
# re-harvest refuses with that card named, instead of reproducing the
# dataset from the wrong inputs.
